[1mNAME[m
       nice - change process priority

[1mLIBRARY[m
       Standard C library ([4mlibc[24m, [4m-lc[24m)

[1mSYNOPSIS[m
       [1m#include[m [1m<unistd.h>[m

       [1mint[m [1mnice(int[m [4minc[24m[1m);[m

   Feature Test Macro Requirements for glibc (see [1mfeature_test_macros[m(7)):

       [1mnice[m():
           _XOPEN_SOURCE
               || /* Since glibc 2.19: */ _DEFAULT_SOURCE
               || /* glibc <= 2.19: */ _BSD_SOURCE || _SVID_SOURCE

[1mDESCRIPTION[m
       [1mnice[m() adds [4minc[24m to the nice value for the calling thread.  (A higher
       nice value means a lower priority.)

       The range of the nice value is +19 (low priority) to -20 (high
       priority).  Attempts to set a nice value outside the range are clamped
       to the range.

       Traditionally, only a privileged process could lower the nice value
       (i.e., set a higher priority).  However, since Linux 2.6.12, an
       unprivileged process can decrease the nice value of a target process
       that has a suitable [1mRLIMIT_NICE[m soft limit; see [1mgetrlimit[m(2) for
       details.

[1mRETURN[m [1mVALUE[m
       On success, the new nice value is returned (but see VERSIONS below).
[7m/tmp/man.XXXXPdBAkL[27m[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       On error, -1 is returned, and [4merrno[24m is set to indicate the error.
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       A successful call can legitimately return -1.  To detect an error, set
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       [4merrno[24m to 0 before the call, and check whether it is nonzero after
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       [1mnice[m() returns -1.
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K[1mERRORS[m
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       [1mEPERM[m  The calling process attempted to increase its priority by
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K              supplying a negative [4minc[24m but has insufficient privileges.  Under
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K              Linux, the [1mCAP_SYS_NICE[m capability is required.  (But see the
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K              discussion of the [1mRLIMIT_NICE[m resource limit in [1msetrlimit[m(2).)
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K[1mVERSIONS[m
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K   [1mC[m [1mlibrary/kernel[m [1mdifferences[m
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       POSIX.1 specifies that [1mnice[m() should return the new nice value.
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       However, the raw Linux system call returns 0 on success.  Likewise, the
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       [1mnice[m() wrapper function provided in glibc 2.2.3 and earlier returns 0
:[K[K [KESCESC[K[[[KBB[K       on success.
